@* AppField.razor — shared reusable field (CLAUDE.md §3.1: used identically
   by Screen A and Screen B), styled through styles/components.css +
   styles/mudblazor-overrides.css, not a .razor.css, since this defines a
   new reusable visual pattern rather than one component's internal wiring.

   ============================================================================
   SUPERSEDES the earlier single-purpose AppField.razor (flagged, not merged
   silently — see below).
   ============================================================================
   The previous version of this file wrapped MudTextField<string> only, with
   Value/ValueChanged/Label/Disabled/Error/ErrorText/AdornmentIcon. Its only
   real call site was Pages/Dashboard.razor:6 (a scratch/demo home page, not
   built-out Screen A/B content yet) — demo/ScreenA/SyncStatus.razor only
   name-drops "AppField" in a comment about future work, it never actually
   used it. Given that, and that this new Type=Text branch below is a
   superset of the old component (same MudTextField<T> wrapper, same
   classes/tokens, T inferred as string for old-style callers), I replaced
   the file in place rather than adding a second, confusingly-similarly-named
   component — updated the one real call site (Dashboard.razor) to add
   T="string" (required — see GENERIC TYPE INFERENCE note below). If you'd
   rather have kept the old file untouched and shipped this as a
   differently-named component, say so and I'll split them back apart.

   ============================================================================
   VALUE-TYPE ARCHITECTURE — why AppField<T>, not one non-generic component
   ============================================================================
   Checked the installed MudBlazor 8.7.0 MudBlazor.xml docs directly (not
   assumed) for all three wrapped controls:
     - MudTextField<T>   : Value/ValueChanged of type T, inherits Error/
                           ErrorText/ErrorId from MudFormComponent<T,T>.
     - MudAutocomplete<T>: same — Value/ValueChanged of type T, inherits the
                           same Error/ErrorText/ErrorId, plus SearchFunc/
                           ToStringFunc/ItemTemplate.
     - MudDatePicker     : does NOT share that lineage. Its base, MudPicker<T>,
                           is its own hierarchy — NOT a MudFormComponent<T,T>.
                           It has no Value/ValueChanged, no Error/ErrorText/
                           ErrorId at all. The bindable value is specifically
                           `Date`/`DateChanged` (DateTime?), fixed, not generic.
   So MudTextField<T> and MudAutocomplete<T> are genuinely generic and can
   share one `T Value`/`EventCallback<T> ValueChanged` pair — that part of a
   single unified component is clean. MudDatePicker cannot share that pair:
   embedding `<MudDatePicker Value="@Value">` inside an AppField<T> would
   only compile for T == DateTime?, not for any T, so it would break the
   Text/Select branches' genericity (or require an unsafe object cast,
   reintroducing boxing right where the brief asked to avoid it).
   Chosen approach: AppField<T> stays generic for Text/Select (T defaults to
   string for virtually all real usage), and Date gets its own dedicated
   `DateValue`/`DateValueChanged` (DateTime?) pair bound straight to
   MudDatePicker's own `Date`/`DateChanged` — Value/ValueChanged are simply
   unused when Type="AppFieldType.Date". This is an intentional, documented
   asymmetry driven by MudBlazor's own API shape (verified above), not an
   awkward compromise invented here.

   GENERIC TYPE INFERENCE: Razor infers T from a bound Value/Options
   expression. If a Text/Select field is used with no Value bound at all
   (e.g. the empty "Default" state in the gallery below), the compiler can't
   infer T and the tag needs an explicit T="string" (or whatever type). Every
   Text/Select usage below sets T explicitly for that reason.

   ============================================================================
   KNOWN GAP — MudDatePicker's rendered DOM is UNVERIFIED for this CSS
   ============================================================================
   CLAUDE.md rule 1: don't guess the rendered DOM. The existing
   mudblazor-overrides.css selectors (.mud-input, .mud-input-outlined,
   .mud-shrink, .mud-input-outlined-border, .mud-input-label-inputcontrol)
   were derived only from MudTextField's shipped CSS text, and were already
   flagged there as not yet DevTools-confirmed. Whether MudAutocomplete<T>'s
   text box and MudDatePicker's text box render through the *same*
   MudInputControl markup (so the same selectors theme them too) is not
   confirmed either way — I have not run `dotnet build` + inspected the
   scopedcss bundle or DevTools Elements for this component yet. Applying the
   same selectors here is a reasoned guess (MudBlazor's picker input visually
   looks identical to an outlined MudTextField with an adornment button,
   which suggests shared markup), not a confirmed fact. Per CLAUDE.md §7,
   treat the Select/Date columns of the states gallery as unverified until a
   rebuild + DevTools screenshot confirms the classes actually land as
   expected — this is flagged again at the top of the CSS section that
   depends on it.

   Separately, because MudPicker<T> has no Error parameter (see above), it
   never gets MudBlazor's own `.mud-input-error` class — so the Error visual
   state for Type="AppFieldType.Date" is driven by AppField's own
   `app-field-input--error` class (added below whenever Error="true",
   regardless of Type), not by MudBlazor's error mechanism. For Text/Select
   this is a harmless, redundant second way to reach the same rule; for Date
   it's the *only* way. Same reasoning for the error message's
   aria-describedby wiring: MudFormComponent's ErrorId plumbs it in
   automatically for Text/Select, but MudDatePicker has no ErrorId, so it's
   added by hand via UserAttributes below.

   ============================================================================
   CORRECTION — "Filled" is not one shared visual state across Types
   ============================================================================
   The first pass at this component treated "has a value, resting" as one
   shared state (reusing the same tokens for Text/Select/Date). Real Figma
   data showed that's wrong: there are two distinct filled looks — Selected
   (blue-accented, Date only — a date has been chosen) and FillOut
   (gray-accented, Text/Select only — has a value). Both render through the
   same MudBlazor .mud-shrink class, so CSS alone can't tell them apart —
   TypeClass below adds a per-Type modifier class
   (app-field-input--date/--text/--select) specifically so
   mudblazor-overrides.css can branch on it. Focused (row 4, "Filled —
   Focused") is unaffected by this split — it's a third, separate state that
   wins over both Selected and FillOut whenever the field has focus,
   regardless of Type, per the confirmed row-3/4/5 reading.

   ============================================================================
   Popups (calendar / autocomplete dropdown): still MudBlazor's default
   indigo/purple theme (screenshots 2 and 3) — not re-skinned, since no token
   values exist for their internals yet. Known gap, not a silent omission.
   ============================================================================ *@
@typeparam T

<div class="app-field">
    @switch (Type)
    {
        case AppFieldType.Date:
            @* No FullWidth here — MudPicker<T> (MudDatePicker's base) has no such
               parameter (confirmed against MudBlazor.xml's full member list for
               MudPicker`1, and by the runtime itself: passing FullWidth="true"
               made Blazor treat it as an unmatched attribute destined for
               UserAttributes, which collided with binding UserAttributes
               explicitly below and threw at startup). MudDatePicker's width is
               already governed by the wrapping .app-field { width: 100% } rule
               in components.css, so nothing is lost by leaving it off. *@
            <MudDatePicker Date="@DateValue"
                           DateChanged="@DateValueChanged"
                           Label="@Label"
                           DateFormat="@DateFormat"
                           Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                           Disabled="Disabled"
                           Editable="true"
                           Clearable="true"
                           AdornmentColor="Color.Inherit"
                           Class="@WrapperClass"
                           UserAttributes="@DateInputAttributes" />
            break;

        case AppFieldType.Select:
            <MudAutocomplete T="T"
                              Value="@Value"
                              ValueChanged="@ValueChanged"
                              Label="@Label"
                              Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                              FullWidth="true"
                              Disabled="Disabled"
                              Error="Error"
                              ErrorId="@_errorId"
                              Clearable="true"
                              ToStringFunc="@ToStringFunc"
                              SearchFunc="@(SearchFunc ?? DefaultSearchFunc)"
                              ItemTemplate="@ItemTemplate"
                              Class="@WrapperClass"
                              UserAttributes="@InputAttributes" />
            break;

        default:
            <MudTextField T="T"
                          Value="@Value"
                          ValueChanged="@ValueChanged"
                          Label="@Label"
                          Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                          FullWidth="true"
                          Disabled="Disabled"
                          Error="Error"
                          ErrorId="@_errorId"
                          Clearable="true"
                          Adornment="@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(AdornmentIcon) ? Adornment.None : AdornmentPosition)"
                          AdornmentIcon="@AdornmentIcon"
                          AdornmentColor="Color.Inherit"
                          Class="@WrapperClass"
                          UserAttributes="@InputAttributes" />
            break;
    }

    @* Same reasoning as the old AppField: never rely on MudBlazor's own
       ErrorText slot (plain string, no icon), so it's never passed to any of
       the three controls above — we render our own icon+text row instead
       (CLAUDE.md §6: never color alone). *@
    @if (Error && !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(ErrorText))
    {
        <p id="@_errorId" class="app-field-error" role="alert">
            <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ErrorOutline" Color="Color.Inherit" Class="app-field-error-icon" />
            <span>@ErrorText</span>
        </p>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// Which MudBlazor control renders internally. Defaults to Text since
    /// that's the shape the earlier AppField always was.
    [Parameter] public AppFieldType Type { get; set; } = AppFieldType.Text;

    /// Persistent visible label (CLAUDE.md §6) — MudBlazor renders this as a
    /// real animated &lt;label&gt;, not a placeholder, for all three controls
    /// (MudTextField/MudAutocomplete via MudInputControl, MudDatePicker via
    /// its own equivalent). There is deliberately no Placeholder parameter on
    /// AppField so a consumer can't substitute one for the other.
    [Parameter] public string? Label { get; set; }

    /// Text/Select value — unused when Type="AppFieldType.Date" (see the
    /// VALUE-TYPE ARCHITECTURE note above for why Date can't share this).
    [Parameter] public T Value { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<T> ValueChanged { get; set; }

    /// Date value — only meaningful when Type="AppFieldType.Date". Bound
    /// directly to MudDatePicker's own Date/DateChanged (DateTime?, not
    /// generic — MudBlazor's own API shape, confirmed via MudBlazor.xml).
    [Parameter] public DateTime? DateValue { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<DateTime?> DateValueChanged { get; set; }

    /// Date-only. Passed straight through to MudDatePicker.DateFormat.
    [Parameter] public string DateFormat { get; set; } = "yyyy/MM/dd";

    /// Select-only. The fixed choice list backing MudAutocomplete.
    [Parameter] public IEnumerable<T>? Options { get; set; }

    /// Select-only. Display text for each option; defaults to T.ToString().
    [Parameter] public Func<T, string>? ToStringFunc { get; set; }

    /// Select-only. Override the default "contains" filter over Options if
    /// the consumer needs different matching (e.g. server-side search).
    [Parameter] public Func<string, CancellationToken, Task<IEnumerable<T>>>? SearchFunc { get; set; }

    /// Select-only. Custom rendering for each dropdown item.
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment<T>? ItemTemplate { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public bool Disabled { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public bool Error { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public string? ErrorText { get; set; }

    /// Text-only adornment (e.g. a warning glyph, or a search icon). Select/
    /// Date have their own built-in adornments (dropdown arrow / calendar
    /// icon) and don't take this parameter.
    [Parameter] public string? AdornmentIcon { get; set; }

    /// Text-only. Which side AdornmentIcon renders on — added for Screen B's
    /// search fields (Icons.Material.Filled.Search at the RTL START, i.e.
    /// the right edge, confirmed against TaskTwo_from_figma_design.png's
    /// pill-shaped search box, CLAUDE.md rule 1), which the original
    /// hardcoded Adornment.End couldn't produce. Defaults to End — every
    /// AdornmentIcon usage before this pass relied on that implicit
    /// behavior, so the default keeps them unchanged.
    [Parameter] public Adornment AdornmentPosition { get; set; } = Adornment.End;

    /// Passed through to the underlying control (e.g. aria-label,
    /// autocomplete) without adding bespoke parameters for each one.
    ///
    /// Defaulted to a non-null, empty dictionary rather than left nullable:
    /// Blazor only populates a [CaptureUnmatchedValues] parameter when there
    /// ARE unmatched attributes on a given usage — when there are none (the
    /// common case; none of the gallery's Select/Date/Text cells pass any),
    /// the property keeps whatever default it was declared with. Left
    /// nullable, that default was null, and null was then handed straight to
    /// MudAutocomplete's/MudTextField's own UserAttributes parameter, which
    /// both dereference internally (a LINQ FirstOrDefault over it during
    /// MudBaseInput.OnInitializedAsync, and MudAutocomplete.GetAutocomplete's
    /// own dictionary lookup) — confirmed by the actual runtime
    /// ArgumentNullExceptions this caused ("Value cannot be null (Parameter
    /// 'source'/'dictionary')"), not a theoretical concern.
    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)]
    public Dictionary<string, object> InputAttributes { get; set; } = new();

    private readonly string _errorId = $"app-field-error-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";

    /// Per-Type modifier class. Exists ONLY so mudblazor-overrides.css can
    /// tell apart the two different "has a value, resting" looks Figma
    /// actually specifies — Selected (blue-accented, Date only) vs FillOut
    /// (gray-accented, Text/Select) — since both render through the same
    /// .mud-shrink MudBlazor class and CSS has no other way to distinguish
    /// them. Applied regardless of state (not just when filled) so the CSS
    /// selectors stay simple compound selectors rather than needing this
    /// class added/removed conditionally.
    private string TypeClass => Type switch
    {
        AppFieldType.Date => "app-field-input--date",
        AppFieldType.Select => "app-field-input--select",
        _ => "app-field-input--text",
    };

    /// Applied to all three controls' Class parameter. The "--error"
    /// modifier is what actually themes the Date branch's error state (see
    /// the KNOWN GAP note above — MudDatePicker has no Error parameter of
    /// its own to drive MudBlazor's usual .mud-input-error class).
    private string WrapperClass => Error
        ? $"app-field-input {TypeClass} app-field-input--error"
        : $"app-field-input {TypeClass}";

    /// MudDatePicker has no ErrorId to wire aria-describedby automatically
    /// (see KNOWN GAP above), so it's added here by hand, on top of whatever
    /// InputAttributes the consumer already passed. Always non-null, same as
    /// InputAttributes itself — copies it into a new dictionary rather than
    /// mutating the original, since InputAttributes is a settable Parameter
    /// a caller might reuse elsewhere.
    private Dictionary<string, object> DateInputAttributes
    {
        get
        {
            var attrs = new Dictionary<string, object>(InputAttributes);
            if (Error && !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(ErrorText))
            {
                attrs["aria-describedby"] = _errorId;
            }
            return attrs;
        }
    }

    private string DisplayText(T item) => ToStringFunc is not null ? ToStringFunc(item) : item?.ToString() ?? string.Empty;

    private Task<IEnumerable<T>> DefaultSearchFunc(string search, CancellationToken token)
    {
        var options = Options ?? Enumerable.Empty<T>();
        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(search))
        {
            return Task.FromResult(options);
        }
        var filtered = options.Where(o => DisplayText(o).Contains(search, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase));
        return Task.FromResult(filtered);
    }
}
